# DESCRIPTION

## Numbers

`strtol`, `strtoll`, `strtoul` and `strtoull` convert the start of <nptr> to an integer.
The subject sequence is white space (space and `\t` to `\r`), an optional sign, and digits in <base>, which is 0 or 2 to 36.
Base 0 reads `0x` as hexadecimal, `0b` (C23) as binary, a leading `0` as octal, and otherwise decimal; bases 16 and 2 accept their own prefix.
A prefix with no digit after it is not a prefix: `0x` converts the `0`.
If <endptr> is not null it is set to the byte after the last one converted, or to <nptr> if nothing was converted (the result is then 0).
A value out of range gives the limit of the type (`LONG_MIN`, `LONG_MAX` or `ULONG_MAX`) and sets `errno` to `ERANGE`.
A <base> that is not 0 or 2 to 36 sets `errno` to `EINVAL` and converts nothing.
The unsigned functions accept a minus sign and negate the value in the unsigned type.
`long` and `long long` are both 64 bits.

`strtod`, `strtof` and `strtold` convert a floating-point number: a decimal or hexadecimal (`0x` with an optional `p` exponent) constant, `inf` or `infinity`, or `nan` with an optional `(chars)`, in either case, after white space and an optional sign.
The point is `.` whatever the locale.
The result is correctly rounded to nearest, ties to even, once; `strtof` is not `strtod`'s result rounded again.
`strtold` yields x87's 80-bit format on amd64 and binary128 on arm64 and rv64, as `long double` is.
An overflow gives infinity, and an underflow that is inexact and gives zero or a subnormal gives that value; both set `errno` to `ERANGE`.

`atof`, `atoi`, `atol` and `atoll` are `strtod` or `strtol` with base 10 and no <endptr>.
`atoi` truncates the `long` to an `int` instead of saturating.

## Memory

The heap is the process's own, from virtual memory objects mapped in its root VMAR.
Blocks of up to 128 KiB less a 16-byte header come from size classes (powers of two from 32 bytes) carved from 1 MiB chunks, and are aligned to 16 bytes; a freed block returns to its class's free list and its memory stays with the process.
Larger blocks, and those aligned more than 16, get a mapping of their own that `free` unmaps.

`malloc` returns a block of <size> bytes, or a null pointer if there is no memory; `malloc(0)` returns a block.
`calloc` returns <count> times <size> bytes, zeroed; the product overflowing is a null pointer.
`realloc` grows or shrinks a block, keeping its contents up to the smaller size; it is in place when the new size fits the block's usable size.
`realloc(p, 0)` frees <p> and returns a null pointer, as glibc's does, and `realloc(NULL, n)` is `malloc(n)`.
`free(NULL)` does nothing.
`aligned_alloc` and `posix_memalign` take any power-of-two <alignment>; `aligned_alloc` returns a null pointer for another, and does not require <size> to be a multiple of it.
`posix_memalign` stores the block in <p> and returns 0, or returns `ENOMEM`; it does not check that <alignment> is a power of two.
`malloc_usable_size` returns the number of bytes a block can hold, as glibc's does: Swift's collections grow into it.

## Ending the process

`exit` runs the handlers registered with `atexit`, last first, writes out `stdout`, and ends the process with <status>.
`atexit` returns 0, or -1 if the table of 64 handlers is full.
`_Exit` ends the process at once, with no handlers run.
`abort` writes `abort()` to standard output and executes a trap, so that the process ends as crashed; no handler is run and no signal is raised.

## Environment and paths

`getenv` returns the value of the variable <name> or a null pointer.
Variables are the files of the process's `/env`, read on each call, as Plan 9's getenv(2) does; a list's elements are joined by blanks.
The string is the caller's to read until the next `getenv` of the same name.
A name beginning `SWIFT_` is read from the process's boot options instead.
There is no `setenv`, `putenv` or `environ` for the program to change.

`realpath` returns the absolute path of <path> with `.` and `..` resolved by name, and fails if it does not exist.
The result goes in <resolved>, which holds `PATH_MAX` (4096) bytes, or in memory from `malloc` if <resolved> is null.
There are no symbolic links to resolve.

`mktemp` and `mkstemp` take a <template_> whose last six bytes are `XXXXXX` and replace them with letters and digits from the system's random generator, until the name is not in use (100 tries).
`mktemp` returns the template with a name not yet in use; `mkstemp` also creates the file, opened for reading and writing with `O_EXCL`, and returns its descriptor.
Without six trailing `X` they fail with `EINVAL`; `mktemp` then returns the template made an empty string.
After 100 tries they fail with `EEXIST`.

## Searching and sorting

`bsearch` searches the sorted array <base> of <n> elements of <size> bytes for <key>, calling <compare> as C asks, and returns the element or a null pointer.
`qsort` sorts such an array in place by <compare>.
It is a heapsort: it allocates nothing, takes O(n log n) at worst, and is not stable.

## Arithmetic

`abs`, `labs` and `llabs` return the absolute value; the most negative value is returned as it is.
`div`, `ldiv` and `lldiv` return the quotient and remainder; a zero divisor faults or traps as the hardware does.

